ne \\(x=n+1\\); if \\(b=n+1\\) it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=n+1\\), which is *not* part of the construction. However, when \\(b=n+1\\) we have \\(a=1\\), and the point \\((1,n+1)\\) belongs to the line \\(x=1\\), which is already present in \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) (if not, we may replace any line of slope \\(0\\) by \\(x=1\\) without affecting the sunny count). Hence every new point is covered, and the covering condition holds for \\(n+1\\).\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{\\,\\text{pres}}(k)\\) is a valid configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Increasing the number of sunny lines by one)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) be as above. Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{\\,\\text{inc}}(k+1):=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{\\ell\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\ell\\) is the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y = \\frac12\\,(x-n) + 1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof that \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}^{\\,\\text{inc}}(k+1)\\) satisfies the required properties.*\n\n1. **Distinctness and sunny count.**  \n   The slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\tfrac12\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny. It does not coincide with any line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) because all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) either have a different slope or pass through a different set of lattice points. Consequently the total number of lines is \\(n+1\\) and the number of sunny lines is \\(k+1\\).\n\n2. **Coverage of the new diagonal.**  \n   As before, the only points that are not already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are those with \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \n   For any integer \\(a\\) with \\(n\\le a\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b=n+2-a\\). Substituting \\(a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac12\\,(a-n)+1 = \\frac12\\,(a-n)+1 = \\frac{a-n+2}{2}=b .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\). The two possibilities are  \n\n   * \\(a=n\\), 