(\\ell_{ns}\\) by a sunny line that still covers all points of the new diagonal.  \n   Choose any integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n\\) and define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{s}: \\; y = \\frac{1}{m}\\,(x-n) + 1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(1/m\\) is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); thus \\(\\ell_{s}\\) is sunny.  \n   Substituting \\(x=n\\) and \\(x=n+1\\) into (2) gives the points \\((n,2)\\) and \\((n+1,1)\\), respectively; substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\((1,n+1)\\). Consequently the three points \\((1,n+1),\\,(n,2),\\,(n+1,1)\\) all lie on \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \n   Every other point of the new diagonal has abscissa \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n-1\\); such a point already belongs to \\(T_n\\) and is therefore covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\).  \n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1}^{\\;{\\rm inc}}(k+1):=\\mathcal L_n(k)\\cup\\{\\ell_{s}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, covers all required points for \\(n+1\\), and contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause from any admissible \\(k\\) for \\(n\\) we obtain configurations for \\(n+1\\) with both \\(k\\) and \\(k+1\\) sunny lines, an easy induction shows that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable. No larger value of \\(k\\) is possible, because a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe detailed proof below carries out the base case, verifies the two constructions, and completes the induction argument.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nFor a positive integer \\(n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Base configurations, \\(n=3\\))