\[\n   y = -x + (n+2),\\; y = -x + (n+1),\\; \\dots ,\\; y = -x + 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   All have slope \\(-1\\) (so they are *not* parallel to the forbidden directions) and are\n   distinct.  For any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b = -a + (a+b)\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); thus the point lies on the line \\(y=-x+(a+b)\\), which is\n   among the chosen ones.  Hence all required points are covered and every line is sunny,\n   giving \\(k=n\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n-1\\)** \u2013  \n   Use the same family as above, but replace the line \\(y=-x+3\\) by the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\).  The set of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=-x+c\\mid c=4,5,\\dots ,n+2\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The vertical line covers the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n\\); the remaining points\n   \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lie on the line\n   \\(y=-x+(a+b)\\) whose constant term belongs to \\(\\{4,\\dots ,n+2\\}\\).  Thus all points are\n   covered, exactly one line (\\(x=1\\)) is non\u2011sunny and the other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny,\n   giving \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=n-2\\)** \u2013  \n   Replace two of the previous lines by a horizontal and a vertical line, e.g.\n\n   \\[\n   \\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=-x+c\\mid c=5,6,\\dots ,n+2\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(x=1\\) covers all points with \\(a=1\\); the line \\(y=1\\) covers all points with\n   \\(b=1\\).  Any remaining point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), so \\(a+b\\in\\{5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and the point lies on the line\n   \\(y=-x+(a+b)\\) whose constant term is at least \\(5\\).  Hence the whole set of required\n   points is covered, exactly two lines are non\u2011sunny and the other \\(n-2\\) lines are\n   sunny, giving \\(k=n-2\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The lower bound \\(k\\ge n-2\\) together with the explicit constructions for\n   \\(k=n,\\;n-1,\\;n-2\\) shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n   \\(\\{n-2,n-1,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\n(and from \\(-1\\)); equivalently it is not parallel to any of the three lines\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound on non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of\n\\(S_{n}\\).  Then at most two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) can be non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe points of \\(S_{n}\\) with maximal sum \\(a+b=n+1\\) form the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n}:=\\{(1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  \nIf a line is non\u2011sunny, its slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nTwo distinct points of \\(D_{n}\\) have different \\(x\\)-coordinates and different\n\\(y\\)-coordinates, so the line through them cannot have any of those three slopes.\nConsequently **each non\u2011sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(D_{n}\\).**  \n\nSince the \\(n\\) lines must cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\), at most two of them can be\nnon\u2011sunny; otherwise we would need more than \\(n\\) lines to cover \\(D_{n}\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Corollary 1\n\nIf a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_{n}\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\nthen  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n}^{(0)}:=\\{\\,\\ell_{c}: y=-x+c\\mid c=3,4,\\dots ,n+2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(\\ell_{c}\\) have slope \\(-1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny.\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and\n\n\\[\nb = -a + (a+b),\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(0)}\\).\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(0)}\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains \\(n\\) sunny lines.\nHence \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n}^{(1)}:=\\{\\,x=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,\\ell_{c}: y=-x+c\\mid c=4,5,\\dots ,n+2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(x=1\\) is vertical, therefore non\u2011sunny; all other lines are sunny.\nThe vertical line covers the points \\((1,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\), then \\(a+b\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\), which is in \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(1)}\\).\nHence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered, exactly one line is non\u2011sunny, and\n\\(k=n-1\\) is realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=n-2\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n}^{(2)}:=\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,\\ell_{c}: y=-x+c\\mid c=5,6,\\dots ,n+2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are respectively vertical and horizontal, thus non\u2011sunny;\nthe remaining \\(n-2\\) lines are sunny.\nThe line \\(x=1\\) covers all points with \\(a=1\\); the line \\(y=1\\) covers all points with\n\\(b=1\\).  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have\n\\(a+b\\in\\{5,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\), which belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(2)}\\).\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n}^{(2)}\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\) and contains exactly \\(n-2\\) sunny lines,\nshowing that \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 any covering family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least \\(n-2\\) sunny lines,\nso \\(k