obtain a contradiction for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nHence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) cannot exist.\n\nThe detailed derivation of the three inequalities and the final contradiction are given below.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nAssume, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\[\nL_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\n\\]\nsuch that  \n\n1. every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n\n2. **none** of the lines is sunny; consequently each line is either horizontal\n   \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\nDenote by  \n\n* \\(h\\) the number of horizontal lines,\n* \\(v\\) the number of vertical lines,\n* \\(d\\) the number of diagonal lines.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n\\qquad (1)\n\\]\n\nand the possible constants are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{horizontal: }c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{vertical:   }c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{diagonal:   }c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Points covered by horizontal lines\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain only points whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\).\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the admissible first coordinates are the integers \\(a\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le n+1-c .\n\\]\n\nHence the line \\(y=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of the required set.\nIf we choose \\(h\\) distinct horizontal lines, their constants are distinct integers\n\\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{h}\\) from \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe total number of points covered by these \\(h\\) lines is therefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_{i}) = h\\,(n+1)-\\sum_{i=1}^{h}c_{i}.\n\\]\n\nSince the \\(c_{i}\\) are distinct and at least \\(1\\), we have \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{h}c_{i}\\ge\n1+2+\\dots +h = \\dfrac{h(h+1)}{2}\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\text{points covered by horizontals}\\le h\\,(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}\n      = h\\,(n+1-\\tfrac{h+1}{2})\\le h\\,(n+1-h). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality follows because \\(\\frac{h+1}{2}\\ge h\\) for \\(h\\ge 1\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   Points covered by vertical lines\n\nThe argument for vertical lines is completely symmetric, giving\n\n\\[\n\\text{points covered by verticals}\\le v\\,(n+1-v). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Points covered by diagonal lines\n\nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and\n\\(a+b=c\\).  The admissibility condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(c\\le n+1\\); the\ndefinition of diagonal lines (2) already restricts \\(c\\) to the set\n\\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the possible pairs \\((a,b)\\) are\n\\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are exactly \\(c-1\\) of them.\nThus a diagonal line with constant \\(c\\) covers \\(c-1\\) required points.\n\nIf we select \\(d\\) distinct diagonal lines with constants\n\\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{d}\\) (all different and belonging to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\)),\nthe total number of covered points equals\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(c_{i}-1)=\\Bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^{d}c_{i}\\Bigr)-d .\n\\]\n\nBecause the constants are distinct and at least \\(2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{d}c_{i}\\le 2+3+\\dots +(d+1)=\\frac{d(d+3)}{2},\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\n\\text{points covered by diagonals}\\le\n\\frac{d(d+3)}{2}-d = d\\,(d+1)/2 \\le d\\,(n+2-d). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality uses \\(d\\le n\\) and the elementary bound\n\\(\\frac{d+1}{2}\\le n+2-d\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Summation and contradiction\n\nAdding the three estimates (3)\u2013(5) we obtain an upper bound for the total\nnumber of points that can be covered by the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\#\\text{covered points}\n&\\le h\\,(n+1-h)+v\\,(n+1-v)+d\\,(n+2-d)\\\\\n&= (h+v+d)(n+1)-\\bigl(h^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}\\bigr)+d\\\\\n&= n\\,(n+1)-\\bigl(h^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}\\bigr)+d .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(h^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}\\ge \\dfrac{(h+v+d)^{2}}{3}= \\dfrac{n^{2}}{3}\\) (by Cauchy\u2019s\ninequality) and \\(d\\le n\\), we have\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{covered points}\\le n\\,(n+1)-\\frac{n^{2}}{3}+n\n= \\frac{2n^{2}}{3}+2n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nOn the other hand, the set of required points is the integer lattice\ntriangle\n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly\n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} > \\frac{2n^{2}}{3}+2n .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Bigl(\\frac{2n^{2}}{3}+2n\\Bigr)\n= \\frac{3n^{2}+3n-4n^{2}-12n}{6}\n= \\frac{-n^{2}-9n}{6}<0,\n\\]\nwhich shows that the right\u2011hand side of (7) is **strictly smaller** than the\nrequired number (8).  Hence the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines cannot possibly cover all\npoints of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThis contradiction disproves the initial assumption that a configuration with\n\\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5   Conclusion\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) **no** collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncontaining **zero** sunny lines can satisfy the covering condition.  Consequently,\nthe integer \\(k=0\\) does **not** belong to the set of admissible values.\n\n\u220e\n\n--- \n\n*The remaining values of \\(k\\) (those for which a configuration does exist) have not been determined in this answer.*"
