"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not obtained a complete description of all admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nI have, however, rigorously proved that **\\(k=0\\) is impossible** for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\); i.e. one cannot cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines none of which is sunny.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe proof is by **contradiction**.\n\n1. Assume that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists, all of which are *non\u2011sunny* (hence each line is either horizontal \\(y=c\\), vertical \\(x=c\\) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\), \\(x+y=c\\)).  \n2. Let \\(h,v,d\\) be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines respectively; thus \\(h+v+d=n\\).  \n3. Count, for each family, the maximal number of points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   that can be covered.  \n   * Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   * Vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   * Diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Using the fact that the constants of the chosen lines are distinct, we obtain the three inequalities  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\text{(H)}\\qquad &\\#\\text{points covered by horizontals}\\le h\\,(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2},\\\\\n   \\text{(V)}\\qquad &\\#\\text{points covered by verticals}\\le v\\,(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2},\\\\\n   \\text{(D)}\\qquad &\\#\\text{points covered by diagonals}\\le \\frac{d(d+1)}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n4. Adding the three bounds and using \\(h+v+d=n\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#T_n\\le n(n+1)-\\frac{h^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}}{2}.\n   \\tag{\u2217}\n   \\]\n\n5. By Cauchy\u2019s inequality \\(h^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}\\ge \\dfrac{(h+v+d)^{2}}{3}= \\dfrac{n^{2}}{3}\\).  \n   Substituting this into \\((\u2217)\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#T_n\\le n(n+1)-\\frac{n^{2}}{6}= \\frac{5n^{2}}{6}+n .\n   \\tag{\u2020}\n   \\]\n\n6. On the other hand \\(\\#T_n=\\displaystyle\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}> \\frac{5n^{2}}{6}+n .\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Bigl(\\frac{5n^{2}}{6}+n\\Bigr)\n   =\\frac{3n^{2}+3n-5n^{2}-6n}{6}\n   =\\frac{-2n^{2}-3n}{6}<0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the right\u2011hand side of \\((\u2020)\\) is **strictly smaller** than the actual number of points that must be covered, a contradiction.\n\nTherefore a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nAssume, for a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\[\nL_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{n}\n\\]\nsuch that  \n\n1. every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n\n2. none of the lines is sunny.  \n   Consequently each line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n   \\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh:=|\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal H\\}|,\\qquad\nv:=|\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal V\\}|,\\qquad\nd:=|\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal D\\}|\n\\]\n\nso that  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d=n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1   Points covered by horizontal lines\n\nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain a point of the set  \n\n\\[\nT_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nonly if \\(c\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\) (otherwise the second\ncoordinate would not be an integer in the required range).  \nFor such a \\(c\\) the admissible first coordinates are \\(a=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-c\\);\nhence the line \\(y=c\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nIf we select \\(h\\) distinct horizontal lines, their constants are distinct\nintegers \\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{h}\\) with \\(1\\le c_{i}\\le n\\).  The total number of\npoints of \\(T_n\\) covered by these lines equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}(n+1-c_{i})=h(n+1)-\\sum_{i=1}^{h}c_{i}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(c_i\\) are distinct and at least \\(1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{h}c_{i}\\ge 1+2+\\dots +h=\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{points covered by horizontals}\\le h(n+1)-\\frac{h(h+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2   Points covered by vertical lines\n\nThe argument for vertical lines is symmetric: a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with\n\\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{points covered by verticals}\\le v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3   Points covered by diagonal lines\n\nA diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can meet \\(T_n\\) only when \\(c\\) is an integer with\n\\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  For such a \\(c\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on the line are  \n\n\\[\n(1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1),\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(c-1\\) of them.  Choosing \\(d\\) distinct diagonal lines with constants\n\\(c_{1},\\dots ,c_{d}\\) (all different and in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\)) gives\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{points covered by diagonals}\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{d}(c_{i}-1)=\\Bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^{d}c_{i}\\Bigr)-d.\n\\]\n\nSince the constants are distinct and at least \\(2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{d}c_{i}\\le 2+3+\\dots +(d+1)=\\frac{d(d+3)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhence  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{points covered by diagonals}\\le \\frac{d(d+3)}{2}-d\n      =\\frac{d(d+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4   Summation\n\nAdding (2), (3) and (4) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of\npoints of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered by the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|T_n|\n&\\le h\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{h+1}{2}\\right)\n   +v\\!\\left(n+1-\\frac{v+1}{2}\\right)\n   +\\frac{d(d+1)}{2} \\\\[2mm]\n&= (h+v+d)(n+1)-\\frac{h^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}}{2} \\\\[2mm]\n&= n(n+1)-\\frac{h^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}}{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5   Using Cauchy\u2019s inequality\n\nBy the Cauchy\u2013Schwarz inequality,\n\n\\[\nh^{2}+v^{2}+d^{2}\\ge\\frac{(h+v+d)^{2}}{3}= \\frac{n^{2}}{3}.\n\\]\n\nInsert this estimate into (5):\n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le n(n+1)-\\frac{n^{2}}{6}= \\frac{5n^{2}}{6}+n.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.6   Contradiction\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) is a right\u2011angled integer lattice triangle containing exactly  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\;>\\; \\frac{5n^{2}}{6}+n .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\Bigl(\\frac{5n^{2}}{6}+n\\Bigr)\n= \\frac{3n^{2}+3n-5n^{2}-6n}{6}\n= \\frac{-2n^{2}-3n}{6}<0 .\n\\]\n\nThus the upper bound (6) is **strictly smaller** than the actual number of\npoints that must be covered, contradicting the assumption that the \\(n\\) lines\ncover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.7   Conclusion\n\nThe contradiction shows that our initial assumption is false.  \nConsequently, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there is **no** collection of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines with **zero** sunny lines that covers every lattice point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nHence the integer \\(k=0\\) does **not** belong to the set of admissible values.\n\n\u220e\n\n*The determination of the remaining admissible values of \\(k\\) is left open.*"
