ns exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   The case \\(k=0\\) gives only the \\(n\\) vertical lines; the case \\(k=n\\) gives only the \\(n\\) diagonal lines; intermediate values are obtained by mixing the two families as described.\n\nCombining the trivial upper bound with the explicit construction yields the full classification of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 2.1  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),\\;(n,1),\\;(1,n)\\).\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=0),\\qquad y\\text{-axis }(x=0),\\qquad x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis, or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) is **not** sunny; all other directions are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2  Trivial upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nWe are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines. Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nTherefore any admissible \\(k\\) must belong to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.3  Lemma (covering by vertical and slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines)\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n> Define the following family of \\(n\\) lines:\n> \\[\n> \\mathcal L_k=\n> \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical, non\u2011sunny}}\n> \\;\\cup\\;\n> \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+d\\mid d=-(k-1),-(k-2),\\dots ,0,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{slope }1,\\text{ sunny}} .\n> \\]\n> Then every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  \n> Moreover \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the diagonal ones) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the vertical ones).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). By definition \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThen the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which belongs to the first part of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a> n-k\\).  \nWrite \\(a=n-k+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k\\).  \nFrom the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a = n+1-(n-k+r)=k+1-r .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\) can take the integer values \\(1,2,\\dots ,k+1-r\\).  \nFor each such \\(b\\) the difference \\(d=b-a\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nd = b-(n-k+r)= (b-r) - (n-k) .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-r\\), the quantity \\(b-r\\) ranges from \\(-(r-1)\\) up to \\(k-r\\). Consequently  \n\n\\[\nd\\in\\{-(k-1),-(k-2),\\dots ,0,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(d\\) is one of the \\(k\\) integers used in the second part of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\), and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(y=x+d\\).\n\nIn both cases the point belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe second part of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of the \\(k\\) lines \\(y=x+d\\) with distinct \\(d\\); each of them has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Hence they are sunny. The first part consists of vertical lines, which are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore not sunny. Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 2.4  Realisation of every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed in the Lemma satisfies all requirements of the problem:\n\n* it consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines;\n* it covers every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* it contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for each such \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 2.5  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved:\n\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) in this interval there exists an explicit configuration (the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of vertical and slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines) that meets the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the complete set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
