st \\(|F|=n-k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n4. **Lower bound on the number of points that must be covered.**  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) contains exactly\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal{P}|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   points (the number of integer solutions of \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\)).  \n\n5. **Deriving the inequality \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   All points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) must be covered by the \\(n\\) lines.  \n   The sunny lines (those not in \\(F\\)) can each contain **at most two** points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\): indeed, a line that is not parallel to any forbidden direction cannot be of the form \\(x=\\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\) or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\); consequently, intersecting the integer lattice in the triangular region \\(\\{a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) yields at most two lattice points (otherwise the line would have to be parallel to one of the forbidden directions).  \n\n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered is bounded above by\n   \\[\n   (n-k)\\cdot 1 \\;+\\; k\\cdot 2 \\;=\\; n+k .\n   \\]\n   Since all \\(|\\mathcal{P}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, we obtain\n   \\[\n   n+k \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) the right\u2011hand side exceeds \\(n+1\\); solving the inequality gives\n   \\[\n   k \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n \\;=\\; \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\; .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\ge 2\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), we conclude that\n   \\[\n   k\\ge 2 .\n   \\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n}\\) the \\(n\\) distinct lines required in the problem statement and let  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{i\\mid L_{i}\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of sunny lines among them.  \nA line is *forbidden* if it is parallel to one of the three directions\n\\(\\{x\\text{-axis},\\,y\\text{-axis},\\,x+y=0\\}\\); let  \n\n\\[\nF:=\\{L_{i}\\mid L_{i}\\text{ is forbidden}\\},\\qquad |F|=n-k .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nA forbidden line contains at most one point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line is horizontal, its equation is \\(y=c\\) with constant \\(c\\).  \nTwo distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in\\mathcal{P}\\) on this line would satisfy\n\\(b=b'=c\\). Then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le n+1\\) give\n\\(a\\le n+1-c\\) and \\(a'\\le n+1-c\\). Since \\(a\\neq a'\\), at least one of them\nexceeds \\(n+1-c\\), contradicting the definition of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  \nThe same argument works for vertical lines (\\(x=c\\)).  \n\nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) for some constant \\(c\\).\nIf two distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) lay on it, they would have the same\nsum \\(a+b=c\\). Because both satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we would have\n\\(c\\le n+1\\). But then the two points would be identical, a contradiction.\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.\nHence its equation cannot be of the form \\(x=\\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\)\nor \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \nSuppose a sunny line contained three distinct points\n\\((a_{1},b_{1}), (a_{2},b_{2}), (a_{3},b_{3})\\in\\mathcal{P}\\).\nThe three points would determine the line uniquely; the differences\n\\((a_{i}-a_{j},\\,b_{i}-b_{j})\\) for \\(i\\neq j\\) would all be scalar multiples of a\nsingle direction vector. Because the three points have distinct integer\ncoordinates, that direction vector would have rational slope, forcing the\nline to be parallel to either a horizontal, a vertical, or a line of slope\n\\(-1\\) (i.e. \\