e three forbidden directions, so each \\(S_t\\) is sunny.  \n- The algebraic description of points on \\(S_t\\) is accurate.  \n- The condition \\(2a+t\\le n+1\\) together with \\(a\\ge1\\) is equivalent to \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-t}{2}\\); this interval contains an integer \\(a\\) iff \\(\\frac{n+1-t}{2}\\ge1\\), i.e. \\(t\\le n-1\\). Hence the non\u2011emptiness claim is justified.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\n#### Step 1 \u2013 Sunny part  \n\n> \u201cTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{0},S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\). They are distinct because they have different intercepts.\u201d\n\n*Correct.* Distinctness follows from different values of \\(t\\).\n\n#### Step 2 \u2013 Points already covered  \n\n> Definition of \\(\\mathcal P_{0}\\) as the union of points with \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n\n*Correct.* This is exactly the set of points covered by the chosen sunny lines, by the characterization in (6).\n\n#### Step 3 \u2013 Forbidden part  \n\n> \u201cEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\setminus\\mathcal P_{0}\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\ge k\\). Consequently \\(a+b\\ge 2a+k\\ge k+2\\). For each integer \\(s\\in\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) define the forbidden line \\(L_{s}:x+y=s\\). Because the sums of the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus\\mathcal P_{0}\\) range exactly over the set (8), each such point lies on a unique line \\(L_{s}\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  \n- If a point is not in \\(\\mathcal P_{0}\\) then its difference \\(b-a\\) is at least \\(k\\).  \n- Since \\(a\\ge1\\), we have \\(a+b = a+(a+(b-a)) \\ge 2\\cdot1 + k = k+2\\).  \n- The maximal possible sum is \\(n+1\\) by definition of \\(\\mathcal P\\). Hence the sums of the uncovered points are precisely the integers \\(s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\).  \n- Each such sum determines a unique line \\(x+y=s\\), which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore forbidden.  \n\nThus every uncovered point is covered by exactly one forbidden line, and the forbidd