            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **forbidden** if it is parallel to one of the three\ndirections \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); otherwise it is\ncalled **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_{s}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), hence forbidden.  \nThe points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) with sum \\(s\\) are precisely the points of\n\\(L_{s}\\) that satisfy the inequalities \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\); therefore the\nfamily \\(\\{L_{2},L_{3},\\dots ,L_{n+1}\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct\nforbidden lines and covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nThus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2  A family of sunny lines\n\nFor any integer \\(t\\ge 0\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_{t}:\\;y=x+t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_{t}\\) is \\(1\\); it is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\), so \\(S_{t}\\) is sunny.\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{t}\\) iff \\(b-a=t\\). Inside the triangle\n\\(\\mathcal P\\) we must also have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the points of\n\\(\\mathcal P\\) belonging to \\(S_{t}\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,a+t)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;2a+t\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for each fixed \\(t\\) the set (6) is non\u2011empty as long as\n\\(t\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) satisfying \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{0},S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k-1}\n   \\quad\\text{(given by (5) with }t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\text{)} .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n2. **Points already covered.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_{0}:=\\bigcup_{j=0}^{k-1} \\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a=j\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   By (6) this is precisely the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) whose\n   coordinate difference belongs to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\n3. **Forbidden part.**  \n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\setminus\\mathcal P_{0}\\) satisfies\n   \\(b-a\\ge k\\). Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\ge (a)+(a+k)=2a+k\\ge k+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\in\\{k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   define the forbidden line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{s}:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the sums of the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus\\mathcal P_{0}\\)\n   range exactly over the set (8), each such point lies on a unique line\n   \\(L_{s}\\) with \\(s\\) given by its sum. The lines (9) are distinct and\n   forbidden.\n\n4. **Counting the lines.**  \n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}+\\underbrace{(n-(k))}_{\\text{forbidden}}\n   =n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, that covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.4  Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nIn any family of \\(n\\) lines each line is either sunny or forbidden, so the\nnumber of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.5  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited:\n\n* a configuration with \\(k=0\\) (Section\u202f2.1);\n* for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration with exactly\n  \\(k\\) sunny lines (Section\u202f2.3);\n* the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) (Section\u202f2.4).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
